    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mindaugas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mindaugas.jpg" alt="Mindaugas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mindaugas">Mindaugas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mindo%C5%ADh._%D0%9C%D1%96%D0%BD%D0%B4%D0%BE%D1%9E%D0%B3_(A._Guagnini,_1611).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alexander Guagnini, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Lithuanian tribes in the forests between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neman">Neman</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neris">Neris</a> remained pagan long after their neighbours converted, organised under dukes whose number is suggested by a treaty of 1219 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> that names twenty-one of them. Pressure from the crusading orders encouraged unification: in 1236 a force of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitians">Samogitians</a> and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> annihilated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-brothers-of-the-sword">Livonian Brothers of the Sword</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-saule">Battle of Saule</a>, the event from which the state is conventionally dated here. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindaugas">Mindaugas</a>, the most powerful of the dukes, eliminated or exiled his rivals and by the 1240s ruled a unified realm. Seeking peace with the Livonian Order, he accepted baptism in 1251 and was crowned king in 1253 with the authority of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-innocent-iv">Pope Innocent IV</a>, the only crowned king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> ever had. His assassination in 1263 by rival dukes ended the kingdom and the first Christian experiment, and a period of instability followed. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/traidenis">Traidenis</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytenis">Vytenis</a> the state regained strength, and around 1316 Vytenis's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminas">Gediminas</a>, who gave his name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminids">Gediminid dynasty</a>, succeeded to a grand duchy that was ready to expand into the lands of Rus' weakened by the Mongol invasions. Gediminas made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> his capital, and in letters of 1323 he invited merchants, craftsmen and clergy from the West to settle in his realm.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/trakai"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trakai.jpg" alt="Trakai" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/trakai">Trakai</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Traku_pilis_by_Augustas_Didzgalvis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BigHead, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The grand duchy was a land of forest, lakes and rivers where most people farmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rye">rye</a>, kept livestock and paid dues in kind to their lords. Its ethnic and religious make-up changed dramatically with expansion: by the fifteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-slavs">East Slavs</a>, ancestors of today's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarusians">Belarusians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a>, formed the large majority, while ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> lived mainly in the north-west. Pagan Lithuanians worshipped deities such as the thunder god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perkunas">Perkūnas</a> and cremated their dead until conversion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> began in 1387 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> proper and in 1413–1417 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas</a> granted privileges to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in 1388 and settled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-karaites">Karaites</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trakai">Trakai</a>, communities whose descendants still live in Lithuania. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">Furs</a>, wax, honey, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, potash and later grain flowed down the rivers to Riga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gdansk">Danzig</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a>. In 1557 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volok-reform">Volok Reform</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a> reorganised royal estates into standard plots, increasing grain output but tightening <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> rights in 1387, grew into a multi-faith city of Catholic, Orthodox and later Protestant churches and synagogues, and by the eighteenth century it was renowned as a centre of Jewish learning, the home of the scholar known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilna-gaon">Vilna Gaon</a>. Noble landowners, Lithuanian and Ruthenian, came to dominate rural society, while towns remained relatively small and many were owned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-vorskla-river"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-vorskla-river.jpg" alt="Battle of the Vorskla River" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-vorskla-river">Battle of the Vorskla River</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Facial_Chronicle_-_b.11,_p.421_-_Battle_at_Vorskla.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anonymous Russian manuscript illuminators, 1560-1570s Facial Chronicle (Illustrated Chronicle of Ivan the Terrible) (..., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>'s rise rested on mobile armies of light cavalry able to raid deep into enemy territory and on the defensive strength of forests, marshes and fortresses such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaunas">Kaunas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trakai">Trakai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>. For more than a century it fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a> and the Livonian Order, answering their annual raids with counter-raids into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonia">Livonia</a>. At the same time it expanded eastwards: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algirdas">Algirdas</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-blue-waters">Battle of Blue Waters</a> around 1362, opening the way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podolia">Podolia</a>, and marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> three times between 1368 and 1372. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas</a> suffered a heavy defeat against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edigu">Edigu</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-vorskla-river">Battle of the Vorskla River</a> in 1399, but in 1410 his troops fought beside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Władysław II Jagiełło</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-grunwald">Battle of Grunwald</a>, breaking the power of the Teutonic Order. From the late fifteenth century the main enemy was Muscovy, which won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vedrosha">Battle of Vedrosha</a> in 1500 and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> in 1514, despite the Lithuanian victory under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konstanty-ostrogski">Konstanty Ostrogski</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-orsha">Battle of Orsha</a> the same year. After 1569 the grand duchy kept its own army under a grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hetman">hetman</a>, and in the seventeenth century its forces fought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a>, suffering a Russian occupation of Vilnius from 1655 to 1661.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/lithuanian-metrica"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lithuanian-metrica.jpg" alt="Lithuanian Metrica" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/lithuanian-metrica">Lithuanian Metrica</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Metryka_litewska.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The grand duke held wide powers as war leader, judge and owner of much of the land, ruling through members of his dynasty and through local princes. Conquered Ruthenian principalities such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> were long left under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminids">Gediminid</a> princes or local dynasties who kept their customs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> faith and Ruthenian law, a flexible approach summed up in a later saying that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> neither changed the old nor introduced the new. Ruthenian, a written <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-slavs">East Slavic</a> language, served as the language of the chancery, whose copybooks of charters and decisions, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanian-metrica">Lithuanian Metrica</a>, survive as a major archive. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-horodlo">Union of Horodło</a> in 1413 the core was divided into the voivodeships of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trakai">Trakai</a>, and Catholic nobles received privileges and coats of arms on the Polish model. The Council of Lords became a powerful body under often absent Jagiellonian rulers, and a general diet developed in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries. The reforms of 1564–1566 created new voivodeships and local dietines, extending political rights to the whole <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a>, Orthodox as well as Catholic. Law was codified in three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statutes-of-lithuania">Statutes of Lithuania</a> (1529, 1566 and 1588), among the most advanced legal codes of their time; the third remained in force in parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> until 1840.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/statutes-of-lithuania"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/statutes-of-lithuania.jpg" alt="Statutes of Lithuania" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/statutes-of-lithuania">Statutes of Lithuania</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_the_Grand_Duchy_of_Lithuania_from_the_Statute_(1614).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The grand duchy shaped the history of four modern nations. For <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuanians">Lithuanians</a> it is the age of independence and greatness; for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarusians">Belarusians</a>, whose ancestors made up much of its population and whose language descends from that of its chancery, it is a formative state of their own; and for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> it is part of a shared past. By drawing most of the western lands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> away from Mongol and later Muscovite control, it created a lasting divide between these lands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, with consequences for later religious and national identities. Its dynastic union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, begun at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-krewo">Krewo</a> in 1385, produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jagiellonian-dynasty">Jagiellonian dynasty</a>, which at times ruled Poland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, and ultimately the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statutes-of-lithuania">Statutes of Lithuania</a> influenced legal thought across eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Cultural life drew on several traditions: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francysk-skaryna">Francysk Skaryna</a> printed books in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> in the 1520s, the first book in Lithuanian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martynas-mazvydas">Martynas Mažvydas</a>'s catechism, appeared at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konigsberg">Königsberg</a> in 1547, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> academy founded in 1579, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius-university">Vilnius University</a>, became a leading centre of learning. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-brest">Union of Brest</a> of 1596, which created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruthenian-uniate-church">Ruthenian Uniate Church</a>, and the long coexistence of Catholics, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, Protestants, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-karaites">Karaites</a> made the grand duchy one of Europe's most religiously diverse states.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/livonian-war.jpg" alt="Livonian War" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siege_of_Narva_1558.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Boris Chorikov, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From 1386 the grand duchy shared a ruler with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> for most of its history, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas</a>, who ruled from 1392 to 1430, kept it practically independent and nearly obtained a royal crown. Growing danger from Muscovy, especially during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livonian-war">Livonian War</a>, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polotsk">Polotsk</a> in 1563, pushed the Lithuanian nobility towards closer union. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a> in 1569 King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigismund-ii-augustus">Sigismund II Augustus</a> transferred <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/podlachia">Podlasie</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/braclaw-voivodeship">Bracław</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crown-of-the-kingdom-of-poland">Crown of Poland</a>, and the grand duchy entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> with a common monarch, diet and foreign policy but its own army, treasury, offices and law. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polonisation">Polonisation</a> of the nobility followed, and in 1696 Polish replaced Ruthenian as the language of administration. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-deluge">The Deluge</a> of 1655–1660 brought Russian and Swedish invasions, devastation and heavy population losses, and in the eighteenth century the grand duchy, like the whole Commonwealth, fell under Russian influence, its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> families such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radziwill-family">Radziwiłłs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sapieha-family">Sapiehas</a> often more powerful than the state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reciprocal-guarantee-of-two-nations">Reciprocal Guarantee of Two Nations</a>, attached to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitution-of-3-may-1791">Constitution of 3 May 1791</a>, reaffirmed its status, but the partitions of 1772, 1793 and 1795 divided its lands, almost all of which went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Grand Duchy of Lithuania grew from a pagan Baltic realm into one of the largest states of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, ruling most of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, and through its union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> it shaped the religious, legal and national history of eastern Europe for more than five centuries.</em></p>

